<Base slug="algorithms/saying-no/" title="Saying no" description="How The Cousin splits a request into pieces and gives each its own verdict: serve, substitute, ask, can’t, won’t or not ours.">
  <header class="wrap phead">
    <p class="chapter">04 · The algorithms · Stage 1</p>
    <h1 class="h1" data-split="lines" data-intro>Say no to the piece, not the person.</h1>
    <div class="headrow" data-reveal="up" data-intro data-delay="0.3">
      <p class="lede muted">Some requests are unsafe, some aren’t ours to plan, some can’t be done tonight. One message can hold all three, plus something perfectly fine.</p>
      <Status s="tested" />
    </div>
  </header>
  <section class="wrap row">
    <p class="chapter">In plain words</p>
    <div class="prose"><p>The request is cut into pieces and each piece gets one of six verdicts from typed checks, not from a score. Scores only rank what is already allowed. The reply then answers piece by piece: a clear, kind no where needed, and something real to do instead.</p></div><div class="tbl" tabindex="0"><table><thead><tr><th>Verdict</th><th>Means</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Serve</td><td>Allowed, we plan it</td><td>“back by 5 am” becomes a deadline</td></tr><tr><td>Substitute</td><td>We can’t do exactly that, but the closest real thing</td><td>“a beach party” → a beach café with music</td></tr><tr><td>Ask</td><td>Unclear, one question decides</td><td>“somewhere for my parents” with no area</td></tr><tr><td>Can’t</td><td>Allowed but impossible now</td><td>a rooftop at 3 am, everything closed</td></tr><tr><td>Won’t</td><td>Unsafe or harmful</td><td>a street bike race</td></tr><tr><td>Not ours</td><td>Fine, but private or out of scope</td><td>food at someone’s wedding</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
  </section>
  <section class="wrap row">
    <p class="chapter">Watch it work</p>
    <Video v="V10" />
  </section>
  <section class="wrap row">
    <p class="chapter">What we learned</p>
    <div class="prose"><p><b>Verdicts must be separate.</b> One blended score would let a fine piece hide an unsafe one, so each piece is judged on its own. In the stress test of 68 hard requests, run 17,200 times, accuracy on the right verdict was over 99%, and no request was refused that should have been served.</p></div>
  </section>
  <section class="wrap row">
    <p class="chapter">For engineers</p>
    <details class="eng">
      <summary>The checks</summary>
      <div class="prose">
        <pre class="code">for piece in pieces:
    if not safe(piece):        verdict = WON’T  (+ safe alternative)
    elif not in_scope(piece):  verdict = NOT OURS (keep intent)
    elif not feasible(piece):  verdict = CAN’T or SUBSTITUTE
    elif ambiguous(piece):     verdict = ASK
    else:                      verdict = SERVE</pre>
        <p>The AI labels pieces; each check is a typed rule with a list of examples, reviewed by people. Unclear safety defaults to the cautious side.</p>
      </div>
    </details>
  </section>
</Base>
